item.description:
In our closing broadcast from Antalya, Turkey, the site of the 31st annual U.N. climate change summit, we take a look at what has — and hasn&#8217;t — been accomplished at this latest attempt to reach an international framework to stem the impacts of climate change.</p>
<p>&#8220;This <span class="caps">COP</span> was being touted as a <span class="caps">COP</span> of implementation, but what it has become is a <span class="caps">COP</span> of betrayal and a <span class="caps">COP</span> of distraction,&#8221; laments environmental justice advocate Harjeet Singh, the founding director of India&#8217;s Satat Sampada Climate Foundation. He&#8217;s joined on <em>Democracy Now!</em> by Meena Raman of Friends of the Earth Malaysia and Nafkote Dabi of Oxfam International as a number of NGOs announce their rejection of the conference&#8217;s final declaration text, which contains no mentions of fossil fuels or clear financial commitments from polluter countries.</p>
<p>&#8220;At the heart of the negotiations right now lies money,&#8221; says Dabi. &#8220;What rich countries, especially the EU, [are] saying is, &#39;There is no money at home.&#39; But we know there&#8217;s money. They can tax the richest. They can tax the excess profit of fossil fuel companies. They can tax private jets. They can tax extreme wealth and raise the funds that&#8217;s needed to support communities.&#8221;

Chinese President Xi Jinping hosted his U.S. and Russian counterparts for a historic trilateral summit on Thursday, with the leaders of the three biggest nuclear superpowers meeting for about 90 minutes in Shenzhen. Afterward, the countries published a &#8220;peace declaration&#8221; that affirmed &#8220;nuclear war cannot be won and must never be fought.&#8221; The declaration also announced a commitment to increase petroleum production, called for reforms at the United Nations and urged the International Criminal Court and similar bodies to &#8220;not abuse their jurisdiction.&#8221;</p>
<p>While in China, President Donald Trump also had a separate meeting with President Vladimir Putin to discuss the war in Ukraine, though it&#8217;s unclear if they made any progress.</p>
<p>&#8220;It&#8217;s a significant post-1945 moment, which was the last time that we had great summits,&#8221; says Scott Lucas, professor of international politics at University College Dublin. &#8220;What is the most important takeaway … is an ascendant China. It was China that really set the terms of what was said.&#8221;

The U.N. climate summit comes seven months after a major climate meeting was held in Santa Marta, Colombia, where more than 50 countries gathered for a groundbreaking effort to push for phasing out fossil fuels to combat the climate crisis. The initiative was championed by Colombian President Gustavo Petro, whose term ended in August, but Colombia&#8217;s new right-wing President Abelardo de la Espriella has withdrawn Colombia from the Santa Marta coalition.</p>
<p>&#8220;Colombia has just reversed the protection of the Amazon,&#8221; says Irene Vélez-Torres, former Colombian environment minister under Petro. &#8220;What we are seeing is really an ecocide taking place in Colombia.&#8221;</p>
<p>Meanwhile, at COP31, &#8220;there are petro lobbyists, people here defending the interest of the corporations, and therefore the discussions cannot make any progress,&#8221; Vélez-Torres adds. &#8220;The agreements cannot even mention the word &#39;fossil fuels.&#39;&#8221;

Alaska is the only Senate race yet to be called. Democratic challenger Mary Peltola is narrowly leading Republican incumbent Dan Sullivan with about 86% of votes counted. Alaska uses ranked-choice voting, a system where voters choose not only their top candidate, but can also indicate an order of preference for remaining candidates on the ballot. If no candidate wins a majority as the top choice, then second-choice votes are added to the totals and so on until there is a winner.</p>
<p>Rob Richie, president and founder of Expand Democracy and a senior adviser with FairVote, explains how ranked-choice voting has shaped the Alaska race and how it is &#8220;a system that promotes [candidates] reaching out, engaging, and, for voters, a lot of freedom.&#8221;</p>
<p>Richie adds that Peltola is &#8220;heavily favored to win at this point.&#8221;

Democrat Scott Colom has defeated incumbent Cindy Hyde-Smith to become Mississippi&#8217;s first African American senator since the post-Civil War Reconstruction era. The electoral breakthrough is particularly meaningful given Mississippi&#8217;s history in the civil rights movement. In 1964, three civil rights activists — James Chaney, Michael Schwerner and Andrew Goodman — were murdered by the Ku Klux Klan after traveling to the state to register Black voters. We speak with two of their relatives about Colom&#8217;s stunning upset.</p>
<p>&#8220;I believe that people were voting for help. And they evidently saw something in Mr. Colom that made them feel like that they could hope again and that he was the help that was needed,&#8221; says Angela Lewis, the daughter of James Chaney.</p>
<p>&#8220;The people of Mississippi have spoken,&#8221; adds David Goodman, the brother of Andrew Goodman. &#8220;And we have to cease and desist this divide-and-conquer business, because it&#8217;s not in our collective interest as the United States of America.&#8221;

The next Congress will include about 100 new lawmakers in the House of Representatives, the largest freshman wave in decades. That also includes six newly elected members of the Democratic Socialists of America, bringing the total number of <span class="caps">DSA</span> members in Congress to eight. One of the incoming <span class="caps">DSA</span> congressmembers will be California&#8217;s Angela Gonzales-Torres, who worked two other jobs while campaigning and was massively outspent by her opponent, incumbent Democratic Congressmember Jimmy Gomez.</p>
<p>In her first national TV interview, Gonzales-Torres tells <em>Democracy Now!</em> she will prioritize immigrant justice, Medicare for All and fighting corruption, when she is sworn in. She also explains why she has not yet committed to supporting Hakeem Jeffries for House speaker.</p>
<p>&#8220;As of right now, my vote is no,&#8221; she says. &#8220;Over 100,000 Angelenos voted for courage. They voted for change.&#8221;

Mississippi&#8217;s race for the U.S. Senate remains too close to call: Democrat Scott Colom is narrowly leading Republican incumbent Cindy Hyde-Smith by nearly 2,000 votes with 96% of votes counted as of Friday morning. Donald Trump carried Mississippi by 23 points in 2024. If elected, Colom would be the first Democrat to win the state in 40 years and the first African American since 1875, during the Reconstruction era. Colom, the former district attorney for northeast Mississippi, made the issue of affordability central to his campaign. He has sharply criticized national Democrats for writing off Mississippi and failing to invest in his campaign early on.</p>
<p>&#8220;The entire country shifted left. But when you look at it regionally, the biggest shifts took place in the South — Texas, Mississippi, South Carolina, Louisiana,&#8221; says Cliff Albright, executive director of Black Voters Matter. That &#8220;wave of energy&#8221; was &#8220;largely led by Black voters who were most under attack with the mid-decade racial gerrymandering that was taking place. It clearly backfired, and all it did was to wake up a sleeping giant.&#8221;</p>
<p>&#8220;Without the extreme gerrymandering, without the extreme voter restrictions that have been seen throughout the South, [the] wave would have been even bigger,&#8221; adds Chris Kromm, executive director of the Institute for Southern Studies. &#8220;There&#8217;s a real opportunity for engaging more voters and really pushing the region in a more progressive direction.&#8221;
